{"t":1789366578,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"f2bf708d-783c-45cd-8ac8-2de095c65c21","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-14T00:34:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 140034\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n834 PM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nUpdated Key Message 1 for latest observations and trends.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Some lingering showers through this evening before turning\n  cooler with less humidity on Monday and Tuesday.\n\n- 2) Increasing precipitation chances later in the week with an\n  incoming cold front.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some lingering showers through this evening \nbefore turning cooler with less humidity on Monday and Tuesday.\n\nAs of 830 PM, radar imagery shows one shower across southern\nPrince William County, and then additional showers and storms\nacross southwestern PA and northern WV. These showers and\nthunderstorms are expected to gradually decay as they move into\nthe area from the northwest over the next few hours. Locations\nalong the Allegheny Front or in the Potomac Highlands may\nexperience a brief shower or rumble of thunder over the next few\nhours. The showers are expected to completely dissipate before\nreaching I-81. Previous discussion follows...\n\nHeading into tonight, an approaching cold front from the Great\nLakes may induce additional showers along and west of the\nAllegheny Front as weakening storms approach from the Ohio\nValley. This threat should end around or shortly after midnight. \nOtherwise, this is largely a drier frontal passage which will \nalso lead to cool temperatures and lower humidity to start off \nthe work week.\n\nGradients on Monday are rather tight for mid-September as a \nbroad 1024-mb surface high approaches from the Great Lakes. This\nwill favor a breezy northwesterly wind for Monday, with gusts \nup to 20 to 25 mph at times. Forecast highs are in the mid 70s \nto low 80s (5 to 15 degrees cooler for mountain locales).\n\nAs the seasonably strong dome of high pressure settles over the\nnortheastern U.S. Monday night, low temperatures plunge into \nthe upper 40s to 50s. The autumnal feel to the air lingers into \nTuesday before an eventual warm up by mid-week. Plenty of \nsunshine can be expected each day, but with decreasing winds on \nTuesday with the ridge along the Eastern Seaboard. This all \ncomes with dew points in the upper 40s to mid 50s which is \ncertainly a change from recent days.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increasing precipitation chances later in the week \nwith an incoming cold front.\n\nUpper-level ridging could continue into Wednesday, then begin \nto break down as a trough forms over the Hudson Bay. A stemming \ncold front tracks east over the Mid-Atlantic during the later \nhalf of the week, bringing increasing humidity as well as shower\n& thunderstorm chances. There is still some uncertainty in the \ntiming of any precipitation, but generally there should be \nincreasing cloud cover by Thursday with a potential for showers \n& thunderstorms between Thursday and throughout the weekend as \nthe front moves through. Severe probabilities continue to look \nminimal for this period.\n\nTemperatures rise back into the mid-to-upper 80s across the region \nthrough Friday, then drop again in the wake of the front heading \ninto the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nHave removed thunder from the TAFs given increasingly shallow \nconvection now producing minimal lightning thanks to increasing\nsubsidence aloft. Otherwise, VFR is expected through Monday \nnight. Winds turn N/NW Monday and gust 20-25 kts.\n\nVFR conditions and lighter winds then continue Monday night through\nTuesday as high pressure builds over the region. Winds shift to\nthe east and southeast Tuesday into Tuesday night.\n\nPrevailing VFR conditions are expected to persist through Thursday \nand Friday. During the afternoon & evening hours throughout this \nperiod, a passing cold front could bring temporary low CIGs at \nterminals as a few showers and/or thunderstorms move through the \nregion. Southerly winds shift northeasterly on Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nA pop up shower or storm can't be ruled out through early this\nevening. A cold front arriving early Monday is forecast to \nyield increasing northwesterly winds with gusts up to 20 to 25 \nknots. Small Craft Advisories have been issued from early Monday\nmorning into Monday night. Multiple wind shifts take place on \nTuesday as high pressure passes by to the north. However, gusts \ndrop off back into the 5 to 10 knot range, generally out of the \neast to southeast.\n\nWinds stay below SCA thresholds through Friday. Southerly winds \nshift northerly by Friday afternoon in the wake of a cold front, \nwhich could bring a few showers and/or thunderstorms over the waters \nbetween Thursday & Friday evening.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTide levels will remain elevated tonight, with Annapolis,\nAlexandria, and Havre de Grace possibly approaching minor.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory from 4 AM to 10 PM EDT Monday for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 4 AM to 10 PM EDT Monday for \n     ANZ530>533-535-536-538>542.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 4 AM Monday to 7 AM EDT Tuesday for \n     ANZ534-537-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/BRO/DHOF/KJP\nAVIATION...LFR/BRO/DHOF\nMARINE...LFR/BRO/DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789366560,"days":{"2026-09-14":{"hi":81,"lo":57,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-15":{"hi":82,"lo":59,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-16":{"hi":86,"lo":64,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-17":{"hi":87,"lo":67,"pop":7,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-18":{"hi":86,"lo":66,"pop":17,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-19":{"hi":83,"lo":67,"pop":26